Coeliac Disease (Adult Onset)
Young Adult & Adolecence
Chronic diarrhoea/steatorrhoea, Positive tTG IgA, Villous atrophy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-007
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of voluminous, dehydrating diarrhea resulting in presyncope and inability to maintain oral hydration.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Housemate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage detailed an acute crisis superimposed on a chronic gastrointestinal history, with the patient experiencing up to twelve episodes of explosive diarrhea in 24 hours, leading to acute postural hypotension and extreme lethargy. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically mandated to evaluate hemodynamic stability, exclude acute infectious enteritis or critical electrolyte derangement, and administer parenteral antiemetics/fluids to avert cardiovascular collapse prior to routine clinic availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I cannot stop running to the bathroom, the smell is awful, and I feel so weak and dizzy I can barely stand up."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a three-month history of non-specific persistent abdominal discomfort and recurrent bloating. Over the past 48 hours, this has violently escalated into severe, chronic episodic diarrhoea with bulky, foul-smelling stools. This acute fluid loss has exacerbated a baseline of generalized fatigue and profound lethargy. The patient reports unintentional weight loss over the past month despite adequate caloric intake. They also describe the recent emergence of intensely pruritic, grouped blisters on their elbows. Every attempt to eat or drink currently triggers immediate bowel urgency.
Functional Status: Currently bedbound; requires physical assistance from the housemate to ambulate to the bathroom due to orthostatic dizziness.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of falls due to acute hypovolemia; patient is exhausted and struggling to cope with the sudden loss of bodily control.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 98/60 (Supine) -> 85/50 (Standing) | HR: 112 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 58 Kg
General: Patient appears pale, cachectic, and acutely lethargic, with dry mucous membranes and delayed capillary refill (3 seconds).
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals generalized distension and hyperactive borborygmi; the abdomen is soft with diffuse, mild tenderness but no rebound, guarding, or palpable masses. Skin examination demonstrates dermatitis herpetiformis characterized by excoriated, intensely pruritic grouped vesicles on the extensor surfaces of both elbows. The clinical phenotype strongly aligns with the diagnostic tetrad of chronic diarrhoea/steatorrhoea, necessitating urgent serological confirmation of a positive tTG IgA and subsequent duodenal villous atrophy on biopsy.
Environmental Observations: A bedside commode is not available; the path to the bathroom is clear of trip hazards.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient has been taking over-the-counter iron supplements for previously diagnosed unexplained iron deficiency anaemia, with no improvement in fatigue.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Exacerbation of Undiagnosed Coeliac Disease with Moderate Hypovolemia - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Campylobacter Enteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of fever, the presence of an unexplained chronic iron deficiency anaemia, and the pathognomonic herpetiform rash on the elbows.

Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Crohn's) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the specific malabsorptive nature of the stool (steatorrhoea) rather than gross hematochezia, and the presence of dermatitis herpetiformis.

Irritable Bowel Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to objective signs of severe malabsorption, including unintentional weight loss, objective orthostatic hypotension, and refractory iron deficiency anaemia.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (High risk for orthostatic falls)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe hypovolemia and presyncope driven by profound malabsorptive diarrhea, superimposed on a chronic history of weight loss, refractory iron deficiency, and a classic vesicular rash. The acute orthostatic hypotension (drop in systolic BP > 20mmHg on standing) and profound lethargy indicated an impending circulatory compromise. Deferring care until the next morning's routine hours would have predictably resulted in severe dehydration requiring emergency hospital admission for intravenous volume resuscitation. The immediate after-hours home visit allowed for urgent hemodynamic assessment, the administration of parenteral symptom control to halt the fluid loss, and the initiation of supervised oral rehydration.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Ondansetron 4mg sublingually stat to control nausea and facilitate oral intake. Administered Loperamide 4mg PO stat to acutely slow gastrointestinal transit. Supervised the initial consumption of 500ml of oral rehydration solution (ORS). Ordered urgent fasting serology (tTG IgA, total IgA, FBC, U&E, Iron studies) to be collected first thing in the morning.
Positive Behavioral Change: To rapidly restore blood volume, consume 50ml of oral rehydration solution every 15 minutes, directly preventing the dizziness and weakness associated with dehydration. To prevent a severe autoimmune flare, maintain a normal, gluten-containing diet strictly until the blood tests are drawn tomorrow, ensuring the diagnostic antibodies remain detectable in the bloodstream.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48 hours to review serology results and arrange an expedited gastroenterology referral for endoscopic biopsy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department via ambulance (000) is required if the patient becomes confused, loses consciousness, stops passing urine entirely for more than 8 hours, or if the orthostatic dizziness prevents them from sitting up in bed.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a presumptive diagnosis of an autoimmune enteropathy (Coeliac disease); definitive diagnosis requires blood tests and a biopsy before committing to lifelong dietary changes.
Clinical Course: The acute fluid loss will stabilize overnight with the prescribed medications, but the underlying malabsorption will persist until the autoimmune trigger is identified and removed.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if you experience a fainting episode, if you become confused, or if you are completely unable to keep small sips of fluid down for the next 4 hours.

